<%#
  Core case page twin of dropdown/cases.html.erb. Same recent-cases list, but these links need
  a real hard navigation - two independent interceptors have to be defeated, not just one:

  1. `turbo: false` instead of `turbo_frame: "_top"`: this frame is nested inside the
     Angular-bootstrapped core page, and Turbo Frames stay navigable even with Drive off (see
     DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md's "Turbo on the Angular case page"), so a "_top" visit would still swap
     the body via AJAX without reloading the script context.
  2. `target: "_self"`: AngularJS's own `$locationProvider.html5Mode(true)` installs a
     document-wide click handler that rewrites any same-origin link lacking a `target` attribute
     into a client-side route change - the same escape hatch every other link in
     _header_core_app.html.erb already relies on (see caseTryNavSvc.js). Without both, Turbo
     correctly steps aside but Angular then intercepts the click itself.

  Skipping either one leaves the URL changing while the page silently keeps showing the old case.

  `class: "dropdown-item"` matches what the original Angular markup put on each of these links
  (dropdown/cases.html.erb, the Rails-page navbar's twin, never had it) - without it the items
  render as plain underlined text instead of full-row, padded/hoverable Bootstrap menu items.
%>
<turbo-frame id="dropdown_cases">
  <% @cases.each do |kase| %>
  <li><%= link_to_core_case kase.case_name, kase, kase.last_try_number, class: "dropdown-item", data: { turbo: false }, target: "_self" %></li>
  <li><hr class="dropdown-divider"></li>
  <% end %>
</turbo-frame>
